WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.880
Hello, I'm Georgie from BBC Learning English,

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:07.040
and in this video we're looking at how to talk about unlikely

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:12.080
or hypothetical situations using
something called 'the second conditional'.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:15.680
Let's get into it.

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:20.800
I asked my BBC Learning English colleagues:

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:24.920
What would you do if you won a million pounds?

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:30.680
If I won a million pounds, I'd probably buy a nice little house in the countryside.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:35.800
If I won a million pounds, I'd probably go travelling all round the world.

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.160
So, we have an unlikely situation - winning a million pounds.

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:42.400
Sadly, that doesn't happen often.

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:47.520
So, the second conditional is used to imagine an unlikely situation

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.800
and think about the outcomes of that.

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:53.960
If George won a million pounds, he'd buy a house in the countryside.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.760
Pippa would go travelling around the world.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.680
Let's hear about another hypothetical scenario.

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:04.040
What would you do if you were alone on a desert island? 

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:07.840
What would I do if I was alone on a desert island?

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:10.520
If I was alone on a desert island,

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:14.000
I think I'd try to find a way to get off the desert island.

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:22.360
If I was alone on a desert island, I think I would be very bad at surviving.

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:25.800
If I were alone on a desert island,

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.760
I would panic because I wouldn't know how to survive.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:35.240
I would feel so lonely.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.000
I would try to find any possible tools.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:43.280
Again, an unlikely situation of being alone on a desert island.

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:45.840
And we're kind of dreaming and imagining:

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.840
what would I do if I were in that situation?

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:52.200
Next, we can use the second conditional to give advice,

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.320
starting with "if I were you...",

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:56.640
meaning 'if I were in your position'

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.320
faced with the same decision or dilemma. Listen to my colleague

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:04.720
give me some advice about a problem.
I've run out of money this month.

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.400
If I were you, I'd ask your parents to give you a small loan

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.880
so you can survive until the end of the month.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:13.800
Again, this is a hypothetical situation.

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:16.400
"If I were you" is actually impossible -

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:18.400
you can't become someone else.

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:20.400
But you're giving advice by saying,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.280
this is what I would do if I were in your position.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.280
Now, pause the video

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:31.120
and think about which structures are used to make a second conditional.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:35.680
The structure is the past simple in the 'if'-clause and 'would'

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:39.280
plus a base verb in the main, or result, clause.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:45.040
To recap, the second conditional is used to imagine an unlikely situation:

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:49.040
If I won the lottery, I'd buy a house in the countryside.

00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:50.200
To give advice:

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.960
It's raining outside, so if I were you, I'd take an umbrella.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:56.080
Don't forget to check out our website

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:59.040
and download the free worksheet to help you practise.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.560
See you next time.

